            {
                "content": "R Plot\nR Scatter Plot\n-->\n\nny:-->\n\n ny:-->\n\nny:-->\n\nR Lists\n\n❮ Previous\nNext ❯",
                "heading": "R Graphics"
            },
            {
                "content": "A list in R can contain many different data types inside it. A list is a collection of data which is ordered and\nchangeable.\n\nTo create a list, use the list() function:\n\nExample\n\n # List of strings\nthislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\n\n# Print the list\n\n thislist\n\nTry it Yourself »",
                "heading": "Lists"
            },
            {
                "content": "You can access the list items by referring to its index number, inside brackets. The first item has index 1, the second item has index 2, and so on:\n\nExample\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\n\n thislist[1]\n\nTry it Yourself »",
                "heading": "Access Lists"
            },
            {
                "content": "To change the value of a specific item, refer to the index number:\n\nExample\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\nthislist[1] <- \"blackcurrant\"\n\n# Print the updated list\nthislist\n\nTry it Yourself »",
                "heading": "Change Item Value"
            },
            {
                "content": "To find out how many items a list has, use the length() function:\n\nExample\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\n\nlength(thislist)\n\nTry it Yourself »",
                "heading": "List Length"
            },
            {
                "content": "To find out if a specified item is present in a list, use the %in% operator:\n\nExample\nCheck if \"apple\" is present in the list:\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\n\n\"apple\" %in% thislist\n\nTry it Yourself »",
                "heading": "Check if Item Exists"
            },
            {
                "content": "To add an item to the end of the list, use the append() function:\n\nExample\nAdd \"orange\" to the list:\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\n\nappend(thislist, \n \"orange\")\n\nTry it Yourself »\n\nTo add an item to the right of a specified index, add \"after=index number\" in the append() function:\n\nExample\nAdd \"orange\" to the list after \"banana\" (index 2):\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\n\nappend(thislist, \n \"orange\", after = 2)\n\nTry it Yourself »",
                "heading": "Add List Items"
            },
            {
                "content": "You can also remove list items. The following example creates a new, updated list without \nan \"apple\" item:\n\nExample\nRemove \"apple\" from the list:\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\n\nnewlist <- thislist[-1]\n\n# Print the new list\nnewlist\n\nTry it Yourself »",
                "heading": "Remove List Items"
            },
            {
                "content": "You can specify a range of indexes by specifying where to start and where to end the range, by using the : \noperator:\n\nNote: When specifying a range, the return value will be a new list with the specified items.\n\n-->\n\nExample\nReturn the second, third, fourth and fifth item:\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\", \"orange\", \"kiwi\", \"melon\", \n \"mango\")\n\n(thislist)[2:5]\n\nTry it Yourself »\n\nNote: The search will start at index 2 (included) and end at index 5 (included).\n\nRemember that the first item has index 1.",
                "heading": "Range of Indexes"
            },
            {
                "content": "You can loop through the list items by using a for loop:\n\nExample\nPrint all items in the list, one by one:\n\n thislist <- list(\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\")\n\nfor (x in thislist) {\n\n print(x)\n}\n\nTry it Yourself »",
                "heading": "Loop Through a List"
            },
            {
                "content": "There are several ways to join, or concatenate, two or more lists in R.\n\nThe most common way is to use the c() function, which combines two elements together:\n\nExample\n\n list1 <- list(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\")\nlist2 <- list(1,2,3)\nlist3 <- \n c(list1,list2)\n\nlist3\n\nTry it Yourself »\n\n❮ Previous\nNext ❯\n\n ★\n\n +1\n\n Sign in to track progress\n\n REMOVE ADS",
                "heading": "Join Two Lists"
            },
            {
                "content": "If you want to use W3Schools services as an educational institution, team or enterprise, send us an e-mail:\nsales@w3schools.com",
                "heading": "Contact Sales"
            }
